  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nH5 ridging continues to push the main storm track well to the\nnorth across the US/Canada border, leaving most of the Rocky\nMountain Region dry and very warm. With temperatures running 10\nto 15 degrees above normal, several record high temperatures are\nin jeopardy this afternoon. While ridging is in place, there is\na weak trough passing by to the north, which will tighten the\nlocal pressure gradient. This will be enough to bring wind gusts\nup near 25 mph across the main wind corridor from RKs to CPR.\nWith dry air already in place, this will bring fire weather\nconditions near critical this afternoon. Red Flag Warnings are\nalready in place, and no adjustments will be needed there. The\nother note with this trough is that there's enough midlevel\nmoisture to spark some virga showers early this afternoon \nacross the northern tier of counties of WY. Chances for actual \nmeasurable precip are very low (<5%).\n\nTuesday will be similar to today, with ridging being the main\nfeature, keeping temperatures well above normal. Winds will be\nlower, as the northern weather system moves east quickly, with\nrelaxing gradient. There is another trough moving into Colorado\nfrom northern California during the afternoon, which may bring\nvirga showers to the southern counties, but again, due to very\ndry surface air, chances for any accumulation remains very low\n(<5%).\n\nThe remainder of the week remains similar, with warm\ntemperatures continuing under the ridge. The next considerable\nshift int he weather pattern still pointing to a deep trough\ndigging along the Rockies this weekend, with colder temperatures\nand a much better chance for precipitation. As noted yesterday,\nmodels have been a bit quick bringing this trough in, with \nbetter chances leaning more toward Sunday.",
